Refactor this code by applying the Interface Segregation Principle to the fat interface:

class ReportPrinter(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def to_html(self): ...
    @abstractmethod
    def to_pdf(self): ...
    @abstractmethod
    def to_csv(self): ...

class HtmlOnly(ReportPrinter):
    def to_html(self): return self.render()
    def to_pdf(self): raise NotImplementedError
    def to_csv(self): raise NotImplementedError

output:
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class HtmlRenderable(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def to_html(self): ...

class PdfRenderable(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def to_pdf(self): ...

class CsvExportable(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def to_csv(self): ...

class HtmlOnly(HtmlRenderable):
    def to_html(self): return self.render()

# clients depending only on HTML never see NotImplementedError paths

def show(report: HtmlRenderable):
    return report.to_html()

What changed: one interface with methods most implementations refused became three small ones; classes implement exactly what they support.

