instruction:
Refactor this code by splitting the fat interface consumed by CLI and worker differently (ISP):

class TaskApi(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def enqueue(self, task): ...
    @abstractmethod
    def cancel(self, task_id): ...
    @abstractmethod
    def heartbeat(self, worker_id): ...
    @abstractmethod
    def progress(self, task_id): ...

class Worker(TaskApi):
    def enqueue(self, task): raise NotImplementedError
    def cancel(self, task_id): raise NotImplementedError
    def heartbeat(self, wid): bus.publish('hb', wid)
    def progress(self, task_id): bus.publish('p', task_id, 100)

output:
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Producer(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def enqueue(self, task): ...
    @abstractmethod
    def cancel(self, task_id): ...

class WorkerApi(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def heartbeat(self, worker_id): ...
    @abstractmethod
    def progress(self, task_id): ...

class Worker(WorkerApi):
    def heartbeat(self, wid): bus.publish('hb', wid)
    def progress(self, task_id): bus.publish('p', task_id, 100)

class CliClient(Producer):
    def enqueue(self, task): bus.publish('q', task)
    def cancel(self, task_id): bus.publish('x', task_id)

What changed: the interface split along actual usage lines; Worker no longer implements (or throws from) methods only the CLI needs.

